mmits whose non-record identity equals their first parent's, peeling any verified refresh it meets to reach one those commits would otherwise hide, and ending at the first commit whose identity differs from its parent's or at its absent-parent and depth bounds - so a branch with no refresh under it still walks. governing_identity composes governing_rev and tree_identity for the branch tip or an explicit revision, including a round's reviewed commit and a trailer's carrier; the branch argument is a branch NAME, because the peel verifies a refresh commit against the branch it names and no refresh subject names HEAD. logged_rounds joins branch-scoped round files to coordinator session logs by train and ordinal, and each round's review phase is the JOINED LOG's declared phase rather than the one its own filename carries, so a file cannot claim a phase its session did not serve; a joined key whose logs declare more than one review phase yields no round. round_entries retains only rounds whose reviewed revision governs the identity under judgement and emits phase, ordinal and verdict entries through an injected parser. declared_phases clamps the configured independent-review count to the available review phases. phases_owed returns declared phases with no drawn entry at the governing identity, independent of whether a drawn verdict parses or approves. round_count returns the largest per-phase entry count at one governing identity, so a multi-phase review cycle counts once and changed work starts a new count. format_trailer and parse_trailer carry `Review-Verdict: APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED rounds=<N> tree=<64 hex>`. branch_trailers verifies each carrier against its governing identity and returns the ordered attestation sequence per tree. Readers treat unavailable repository answers according to their own fail-closed contract.\"\"\"\n+code_symbol = \"RECORD_PREFIXES/fold_listing/tree_identity/refresh_subject/refresh_attestation/mechanical_close_attestation/work_tip/governing_rev/governing_identity/format_trailer/parse_trailer/round_file/session_log/branch_paths/logged_rounds/round_entries/branch_entries/declared_phases/phases_owed/round_count/format_branch_trailer/branch_trailers\"\n+detail = \"\"\"tree_identity folds raw NUL-delimited `git ls-tree -r -z` entries after dropping docs/reviews/, docs/log.d/ and docs/iteration/, and returns a 64-hex SHA-256 identity. fold_listing accepts bytes and preserves every path byte so display quoting or decoding cannot merge distinct work trees. refresh_attestation validates a station refresh from its subject, first parent, commit tree and Bar-Green trailer. work_tip peels only a verified refresh at the tip for the reset path; governing_rev is read-only and walks across commits whose non-record identity equals their first parent's, peeling any DISPOSABLE commit it meets to reach one those commits would otherwise hide, and ending at the first commit whose identity differs from its parent's or at its absent-parent and depth bounds - so a branch with no disposable commit under it still walks. TWO commit classes are disposable and the read-only walk peels BOTH: the station's REFRESH, admitted by refresh_attestation, and the machinery's OWN adjudication close, admitted by mechanical_close_attestation against exactly one parent and a NON-EMPTY no-renames changed-path stream lying wholly under docs/work/. That stream must pair deleted specs from one docs/work/active/<branch>/ with additions of the same filenames under docs/work/complete/; their canonically ordered filename WI ids are passed through the writer's mechanical_close_subject composer and the result must equal the commit subject exactly, so a public prefix/suffix with a forged middle is not an attestation. One property admits the two classes and it is not two rules: both are machine-authored end to end, and both move the tree without the lane having changed what it CLAIMS - docs/work/ is deliberately inside the identity because a spec's own claims are what a verdict is about, and the close carries those claims through unchanged. Every arm of both verifications fails toward REVIEW, since declining to peel measures at a later rev and can never ask for LESS - though not always for more: on the empty-path arm the refused commit is identity-preserving, so the walk-through step reaches the same rev the peel would have, and the clause guards this function's contract rather than any answer the gate gives. The two peels are NOT the same peel: work_tip calls refresh_attestation directly rather than through the shared disposable test, so a mechanical close is never removed by the destructive reset path, which may only ever drop a refresh sitting literally on the tip. governing_identity composes governing_rev and tree_identity for the branch tip or an explicit revision, including a round's reviewed commit and a trailer's carrier; the branch argument is a branch NAME, because the peel verifies a refresh commit against the branch it names and no refresh subject names HEAD. logged_rounds joins branch-scoped round files to coordinator session logs by train and ordinal, and each round's review phase is the JOINED LOG's declared phase rather than the one its own filename carries, so a file cannot claim a phase its session did not serve; a joined key whose logs declare more than one review phase yields no round. round_entries retains only rounds whose reviewed revision governs the identity under judgement and emits phase, ordinal and verdict entries through an injected parser. declared_phases clamps the configured independent-review count to the available review phases. phases_owed returns declared phases with no drawn entry at the governing identity, independent of whether a drawn verdict parses or approves. round_count returns the largest per-phase entry count at one governing identity, so a multi-phase review cycle counts once and changed work starts a new count. format_trailer and parse_trailer carry `Review-Verdict: APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED rounds=<N> tree=<64 hex>`. branch_trailers verifies each carrier against its governing identity and returns the ordered attestation sequence per tree. Readers treat unavailable repository answers according to their own fail-closed contract.\"\"\"\n rationale = \"\"\"The merge gate asks whether a lane may merge while the loop asks which review phase the lane still owes. A shared identity, evidence join, phase span and count keep both answers on the same tree and policy. Logged round evidence is authoritative because its coordinator session establishes reviewer provenance; the self-verifying trailer is only a cross-check and cannot create an approval. Identity binding answers staleness directly and prevents ordering from promoting a verdict for different work. Separate read-only and reset peels protect both contracts: the reader may traverse identity-preserving commits, while the destructive reset path may remove only the verified refresh at its tip.\"\"\"\n test_refs = \"TC-205\"\n status = \"Drafted\"\n@@ -2195,8 +2195,8 @@ phase = 5\n sr_refs = [\"SR-170\"]\n title = \"The per-review-scope verdict rollup: generated, checked, never an input\"\n module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/gen_verdict_rollup.py\"\n-code_symbol = \"scopes/render/targets/_extra/main\"\n-detail = \"\"\"One file per review scope under docs/reviews/rollup/<train>.md, compiled from the round files that scope carries: ordinal, phase, reviewed sha, parsed verdict word, finding count and filename, one row each. The scope SET is the train field the round-file name parser itself returns, taken over every round file found under docs/reviews/ - not the directory layout, because a second notion of what a review scope is can disagree with the first: enumerating directories skipped the flat pre-train layout that parser supports, which produced an empty rollup directory AND a --check calling it fresh against the same empty target set. That layout has no directory to be named after, so its rollup takes a reserved stem, and the one name collision that leaves is REFUSED by name on both the check and the write arm rather than resolved by a last write - two scopes sharing one output file leave the other permanently stale. The scope is the TRAIN and not the work item, because a traincar is one review scope - a worker schedules its round only once every assigned item is built and the round covers the combined train diff, so a per-item rollup would have to invent a slice of a round that does not exist. Declared in the toolchain file's generated section, freshness-gated by --check at the commit floor, and standing down on a work branch with the rest of the trunk-owned artifacts - the one member of that set whose SOURCE a branch necessarily writes, since the round files are a lane's own committed evidence. THE WRITER THAT CLOSES THAT CIRCLE IS THE TRUNK STEP'S REGEN SET, named by the id it carries there: the `verdict-rollup` row, armed by the presence of docs/reviews/ and skipped with a printed notice wherever that directory is absent, a LEAF whose position in the dependency order is therefore free. Membership in that set is part of THIS contract and not an implementation accident, because it is the only thing that makes the exclusive-writer clause above true: a work branch stands the freshness check down and commits the round files, so a trunk step that does not run this generator leaves the artifact written by nobody, and the first commit floor that reads it reds on a staleness no lane is permitted to clear. The generator OWNS that directory rather than only writing into it: --check reports an absent file, a diverged one and an EXTRA one - a retired review scope keeping its rollup is the same lie as an out-of-date one - on normalized line endings, and the WRITE path prunes the extra in the pass that writes the rest. Both halves or neither: reporting a file the regenerator would never touch made the remedy the failure message names unable to clear the failure, which on a step sitting at the commit floor and in the trunk freshness set is an unbreakable red under a misleading instruction. It names NO governing verdict: governing is tree identity, a question about a branch's current tree that can only be answered against a repository at a moment, so every row here is a fact about a file.\"\"\"\n+code_symbol = \"scopes/render/targets/_extra/_off_trunk_refusal/main\"\n+detail = \"\"\"One file per review scope under docs/reviews/rollup/<train>.md, compiled from the round files that scope carries: ordinal, phase, reviewed sha, parsed verdict word, finding count and filename, one row each. The scope SET is the train field the round-file name parser itself returns, taken over every round file found under docs/reviews/ - not the directory layout, because a second notion of what a review scope is can disagree with the first: enumerating directories skipped the flat pre-train layout that parser supports, which produced an empty rollup directory AND a --check calling it fresh against the same empty target set. That layout has no directory to be named after, so its rollup takes a reserved stem, and the one name collision that leaves is REFUSED by name on both the check and the write arm rather than resolved by a last write - two scopes sharing one output file leave the other permanently stale. The scope is the TRAIN and not the work item, because a traincar is one review scope - a worker schedules its round only once every assigned item is built and the round covers the combined train diff, so a per-item rollup would have to invent a slice of a round that does not exist. Declared in the toolchain file's generated section, freshness-gated by --check at the commit floor, and standing down on a work branch with the rest of the trunk-owned artifacts - the one member of that set whose SOURCE a branch necessarily writes, since the round files are a lane's own committed evidence. THE WRITER THAT CLOSES THAT CIRCLE IS THE TRUNK STEP'S REGEN SET, named by the id it carries there: the `verdict-rollup` row, armed by the presence of docs/reviews/ and skipped with a printed notice wherever that directory is absent, a LEAF whose position in the dependency order is therefore free. TWO mechanisms hold the exclusive-writer clause above and they hold DIFFERENT halves of it, so neither substitutes for the other. What ENFORCES it is `_off_trunk_refusal`: a direct write is refused with exit 2 whenever the checkout sits on a branch other than the trunk (agent_common.trunk_name, the PRIMARY checkout's branch, so a single-checkout repo is its own trunk and an attended run is unaffected), with exactly one caller exempt - the trunk step's own `--trunk-step`, because the station's refresh runs the trunk step INSIDE the lane worktree, where the branch is a work branch and the writer is still the trunk step. --check never writes and is never refused. The refusal is load-bearing rather than belt-and-braces: without it the generator returned 0 and wrote the directory in a claimed lane, and the work-branch freshness stand-down then HID the write, which is the one failure this file's stand-down cannot report. What keeps the artifact FRESH is membership in that regen set, which is part of THIS contract and not an implementation accident: a work branch stands the freshness check down and commits the round files, so a trunk step that does not run this generator leaves the artifact written by nobody, and the first commit floor that reads it reds on a staleness no lane is permitted to clear. The generator OWNS that directory rather than only writing into it: --check reports an absent file, a diverged one and an EXTRA one - a retired review scope keeping its rollup is the same lie as an out-of-date one - on normalized line endings, and the WRITE path prunes the extra in the pass that writes the rest. Both halves or neither: reporting a file the regenerator would never touch made the remedy the failure message names unable to clear the failure, which on a step sitting at the commit floor and in the trunk freshness set is an unbreakable red under a misleading instruction. It names NO governing verdict: governing is tree identity, a question about a branch's current tree that can only be answered against a repository at a moment, so every row here is a fact about a file.\"\"\"\n rationale = \"\"\"The hand-authored per-item rollup was the merge gate's only input and nothing in the kit wrote it, so every mechanized lane stopped at a human - and because it paraphrased evidence that already existed, it was also the one artifact in the tree that was neither generated, checked nor declared while the doctrine everywhere else refuses that shape. Splitting the roles is what makes both halves honest: the gate reads the evidence, and the summary a human genuinely wants to read becomes derived state that cannot drift from it. Writing a governing verdict into the file would rebuild the retired artifact under a new name - a checked-in summary that reads as authority and is stale the moment the branch moves - so the file states facts about files and stops.\"\"\"\n test_refs = \"TC-206\"\n status = \"Approved\"\ndiff --git a/docs/test/test-cases.toml b/docs/test/test-cases.toml\nindex 14c4723a..ed448aeb 100644\n--- a/docs/test/test-cases.toml\n+++ b/docs/test/test-cases.toml\n@@ -2080,22 +2080,22 @@ phase = 5\n [test.TC-205]\n verifies = [\"SR-156\", \"LLR-207\", \"IF-175\"]\n level = \"Unit\"\n-method = \"\"\"The verdict record's two halves, each driven beside its opposite so neither reads as a rule with one answer. THE IDENTITY, pure over synthetic `git ls-tree -r` entries: a listing and the same listing plus a round file, a log fragment and a session log fold EQUAL, while a changed work blob and a changed docs/work/ spec each fold DIFFERENT - so the exclusion is proven to be an exclusion rather than a fold that ignores everything. THE ENCODING BOUNDARY, driven through git rather than asserted about it: 